Q: How to fix &quot;Error: JavaFX runtime components are missing, and are required to run this application&quot;? A: <p>Create a modular application.</p>
<p><code>module-info.java</code> should exist</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>module javafx.reproducer {
    requires javafx.controls;
    requires javafx.fxml;

    opens org.jabreftest.test.javafxreproducer to javafx.fxml;
    exports org.jabreftest.test.javafxreproducer;
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>plugins {
    java
    application
    id(&quot;org.gradlex.extra-java-module-info&quot;) version &quot;1.14&quot;
    id(&quot;org.gradlex.jvm-dependency-conflict-resolution&quot;) version &quot;2.5&quot;
    id(&quot;org.gradlex.java-module-dependencies&quot;) version &quot;1.12&quot;
    id(&quot;org.gradlex.java-module-packaging&quot;) version &quot;1.2&quot;
}

group = &quot;org.jabreftest.test&quot;

// &quot;1.0-SNAPSHOT&quot; cannot be used; otherwise we get
//   java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: Version [1.0-SNAPSHOT] contains invalid component [0-SNAPSHOT]
// 0.1.0 cannaot be used; otherwise we get
//   Bundler Mac Application Image skipped because of a configuration problem: The first number in an app-version cannot be zero or negative.
version = &quot;1.0.0&quot;

repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}

val javafx = &quot;25.0.2&quot;
val junitVersion = &quot;6.0.2&quot;

java {
  toolchain {
    languageVersion.set(JavaLanguageVersion.of(25))
  }
}

tasks.withType&lt;JavaCompile&gt;().configureEach {
    options.encoding = &quot;UTF-8&quot;
}

application {
    mainModule.set(&quot;javafx.reproducer&quot;)
    mainClass.set(&quot;org.jabreftest.test.javafxreproducer.HelloApplication&quot;)
}

dependencies {
    implementation(&quot;org.openjfx:javafx-controls:$javafx&quot;)
    implementation(&quot;org.openjfx:javafx-fxml:$javafx&quot;)
    testImplementation(&quot;org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter-api:$junitVersion&quot;)
    testRuntimeOnly(&quot;org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter-engine:$junitVersion&quot;)
}

tasks.test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}

jvmDependencyConflicts.patch {
    listOf(&quot;base&quot;, &quot;controls&quot;, &quot;fxml&quot;, &quot;graphics&quot;).forEach { jfxModule -&gt;
        module(&quot;org.openjfx:javafx-$jfxModule&quot;) {
            addTargetPlatformVariant(&quot;&quot;, &quot;none&quot;, &quot;none&quot;) // matches the empty Jars: to get better errors
            addTargetPlatformVariant(&quot;linux&quot;, OperatingSystemFamily.LINUX, MachineArchitecture.X86_64)
            addTargetPlatformVariant(&quot;linux-aarch64&quot;, OperatingSystemFamily.LINUX, MachineArchitecture.ARM64)
            addTargetPlatformVariant(&quot;mac&quot;, OperatingSystemFamily.MACOS, MachineArchitecture.X86_64)
            addTargetPlatformVariant(&quot;mac-aarch64&quot;, OperatingSystemFamily.MACOS, MachineArchitecture.ARM64)
            addTargetPlatformVariant(&quot;win&quot;, OperatingSystemFamily.WINDOWS, MachineArchitecture.X86_64)
        }
    }
}

extraJavaModuleInfo {
    failOnAutomaticModules = true
    failOnModifiedDerivedModuleNames = true
    skipLocalJars = true
}

// Source: https://github.com/jjohannes/java-module-system/blob/main/gradle/plugins/src/main/kotlin/targets.gradle.kts
// Configure variants for OS. Target name can be any string, but should match the name used in GitHub actions.
javaModulePackaging {
    vendor = &quot;JabRef&quot;
    jlinkOptions.addAll(
        &quot;--strip-debug&quot;, &quot;--compress&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, &quot;--no-header-files&quot;, &quot;--no-man-pages&quot;
    )

    target(&quot;ubuntu-22.04&quot;) {
        operatingSystem = OperatingSystemFamily.LINUX
        architecture = MachineArchitecture.X86_64
        packageTypes = listOf(&quot;app-image&quot;, &quot;deb&quot;, &quot;rpm&quot;)
    }
    target(&quot;ubuntu-22.04-arm&quot;) {
        operatingSystem = OperatingSystemFamily.LINUX
        architecture = MachineArchitecture.ARM64
        packageTypes = listOf(&quot;app-image&quot;, &quot;deb&quot;, &quot;rpm&quot;)
    }
    target(&quot;macos-13&quot;) {
        operatingSystem = OperatingSystemFamily.MACOS
        architecture = MachineArchitecture.X86_64
        packageTypes = listOf(&quot;app-image&quot;, &quot;dmg&quot;, &quot;pkg&quot;)
        singleStepPackaging = true
    }
    target(&quot;macos-14&quot;) {
        operatingSystem = OperatingSystemFamily.MACOS
        architecture = MachineArchitecture.ARM64
        packageTypes = listOf(&quot;app-image&quot;, &quot;dmg&quot;, &quot;pkg&quot;)
        singleStepPackaging = true
    }
    target(&quot;windows-latest&quot;) {
        operatingSystem = OperatingSystemFamily.WINDOWS
        architecture = MachineArchitecture.X86_64
        packageTypes = listOf(&quot;app-image&quot;, &quot;msi&quot;)
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p>Full example: <a href="https://github.com/Siedlerchr/javafxreproducer/tree/main" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/Siedlerchr/javafxreproducer/tree/main</a></p>

Q: Why cryptopp generates different signature than libsecp256k1 for ECDSA A: <p><strong>TL;DR</strong> - Both implementations use deterministic ECDSA as specified in <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6979" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RFC 6979</a>. The reason for the different results is that, unlike <em>libsecp256k1</em>, <em>Crypto++</em> implicitly hashes as well as does not normalize the <code>s</code> part.</p>
<p>If the implicit hashing is omitted and <code>s</code> is normalized, the same signature can be produced as with <em>libsecp256k1</em>.</p>
<h3>Required changes to disable implicit hashing:</h3>
<p>Before a message is signed, it is first hashed. <em>libsecp256k1</em> expects the message hash instead of the message. This has the advantage that even messages for which only their hash is known can be signed. <em>Crypto++</em>, on the other hand, expects the message itself and performs the hashing itself.</p>
<p>The <em>Crypto++</em> documentation provides a workaround to skip the implicit hashing, see section <em><a href="https://www.cryptopp.com/wiki/Elliptic_Curve_Digital_Signature_Algorithm#Precomputed_Hashes" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Precomputed Hashes</a></em>. Here, an <code>IdentityHash</code> is defined whose output simply corresponds to the input (i.e., the input is effectively not hashed). However, this workaround only works for <code>ECDSA&lt;EC,H&gt;</code>, but not for the deterministic variant <code>ECDSA_RFC6979&lt;EC,H&gt;</code>. For the latter, the hash <code>H</code> is used not only for hashing the message, but also for the HMAC, which is involved in determining <em>k</em>. With the HMAC, however, <code>IdentityHash</code> must not be used; instead, the actual hash function (e.g., <code>SHA256</code>) must be applied. Therefore, for <code>ECDSA_RFC6979&lt;EC,H&gt;</code>, it is necessary to specify two hash functions:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class EC, class H, class H_IDENTITY&gt;
struct ECDSA_RFC6979_IDENTITY : public DL_SS&lt;
    DL_Keys_ECDSA&lt;EC&gt;,
    DL_Algorithm_ECDSA_RFC6979&lt;EC, H&gt;,
    DL_SignatureMessageEncodingMethod_DSA,
    H_IDENTITY,
    ECDSA_RFC6979_IDENTITY&lt;EC, H, H_IDENTITY&gt; &gt;
{
    static std::string CRYPTOPP_API StaticAlgorithmName() { return std::string(&quot;ECDSA-RFC6979/&quot;) + H::StaticAlgorithmName(); }
};
</code></pre>
<p>where <code>H_IDENTITY</code> is a placeholder for the <code>IdentityHash</code> type, which is defined as in the <em>Crypto++</em> documentation, section <em><a href="https://www.cryptopp.com/wiki/Elliptic_Curve_Digital_Signature_Algorithm#Precomputed_Hashes" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Precomputed Hashes</a></em>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;unsigned int HASH_SIZE = 32&gt;
class IdentityHash : public HashTransformation
{
public:
    CRYPTOPP_CONSTANT(DIGESTSIZE = HASH_SIZE);
    static const char * StaticAlgorithmName()
    {
        return &quot;IdentityHash&quot;;
    }

    IdentityHash() : m_digest(HASH_SIZE), m_idx(0) {}

    virtual unsigned int DigestSize() const
    {
        return DIGESTSIZE;
    }

    virtual void Update(const byte *input, size_t length)
    {
        size_t s = STDMIN(STDMIN&lt;size_t&gt;(DIGESTSIZE, length),
                                         DIGESTSIZE - m_idx);    
        if (s)
            ::memcpy(&amp;m_digest[m_idx], input, s);
        m_idx += s;
    }

    virtual void TruncatedFinal(byte *digest, size_t digestSize)
    {
        ThrowIfInvalidTruncatedSize(digestSize);

        if (m_idx != DIGESTSIZE)
            throw Exception(Exception::OTHER_ERROR, &quot;Input size must be &quot; + IntToString((int)DIGESTSIZE));

        if (digest)
            ::memcpy(digest, m_digest, digestSize);

        m_idx = 0;
    }

private:
    SecByteBlock m_digest;
    size_t m_idx;
};
</code></pre>
<h3>Required changes for normalization:</h3>
<p>Ethereum uses canonical signatures and therefore performs normalization if necessary, i.e., for <code>s &gt; n/2</code>, the value <code>s' = n - s</code> is used instead of <code>s</code> (<code>n</code> is the order of the generator point), see e.g., <em><a href="https://denispopovengineer.medium.com/low-s-restriction-in-elliptic-curve-signatures-02febc036b47" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Low-S Restriction in Elliptic Curve Signatures</a></em>. This can be achieved, e.g., with:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void normalize(std::string &amp;s) {

    typedef DL_GroupParameters_EC&lt;ECP&gt; GroupParameters;
    GroupParameters group;
    group.Initialize(ASN1::secp256k1());
    Integer order = group.GetGroupOrder(); 
    Integer half_order = order.DividedBy(Integer(&quot;2&quot;));

    Integer s_int((unsigned char*)s.data(), s.size());
    if (s_int.Compare(half_order) &gt; 0) {
        Integer s_normalized_int = order - s_int;
        size_t len = s_normalized_int.MinEncodedSize(Integer::UNSIGNED);
        std::vector&lt;byte&gt; v(len);
        s_normalized_int.Encode((byte*)&amp;v[0], v.size(), Integer::UNSIGNED);
        s = std::string(v.begin(), v.end());
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Use case (based on your code using the changes described above):</h3>
<p>Using the definitions above, the following use case:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void sign_cryptopp() {
    Integer x(seckey, 32);
    ECDSA_RFC6979_IDENTITY&lt;ECP, SHA256, IdentityHash&lt;32&gt;&gt;::PrivateKey priv_key;
    priv_key.Initialize(ASN1::secp256k1(), x);

    ECDSA_RFC6979_IDENTITY&lt;ECP, SHA256, IdentityHash&lt;32&gt;&gt;::Signer signer(priv_key);
    signer.AccessKey() = priv_key;
    auto sigLen = signer.MaxSignatureLength();
    std::string signature(sigLen, 0x00);
    signer.SignMessage(NullRNG(), msg_hash, 32, (byte*)signature.data());

    std::string r = signature.substr(0, 32);
    std::string s = signature.substr(32, 32);
    normalize(s);

    std::string rhex{};
    std::string shex{};
    StringSource(r, true, new HexEncoder(new StringSink(rhex), false));
    StringSource(s, true, new HexEncoder(new StringSink(shex), false));

    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;r: &quot; &lt;&lt; rhex &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;s: &quot; &lt;&lt; shex &lt;&lt; std::endl;
}
</code></pre>
<p>produces the following output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>r: ff05eca1dd065434a2d559f3873885c2a210f7278b5361bb1a8c4794b57e424c
s: 61561c4a01a102d806adac2ae61aae87054229e86b6be9c667e0830c144ee0a7
</code></pre>
<p>analogous to the calculation with <em>libsecp256k1</em>.</p>
<p>Tested with Crypto++ v8.9.0.</p>
<hr />
<p>A note on the recovery ID or <code>v</code>:</p>
<p><a href="https://bitcoin.stackexchange.com/a/38909">This post</a> explains the purpose of the recovery ID and the relationship between the recovery ID and <code>v</code>.</p>
<p>The recovery ID can be determined relatively easily during signing. It becomes more complicated if it has to be done retrospectively using the signature <code>(r,s)</code>. Since <em>Crypto++</em> encapsulates signing, the only option is probably to determine it using <code>(r,s)</code> if you do not want to reimplement signing with the support of the recovery ID. The algorithm for determining the recovery ID is basically described <a href="https://www.secg.org/sec1-v2.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> in <em>section 4.1.6</em> and can of course be implemented with <em>Crypto++</em>. If this is needed, it should be addressed in a separate question, as it is beyond the scope of this question.</p>

Q: Splitting a string into tokens with several possible separators, using `std::ranges` A: <p>Out of curiosity, here is another proposal based on <code>std::views::split</code> that can be quite fast (based on C++23 though).</p>
<p>Actually, the annoying part is to deal with several separators (mainly for performance), so the idea here is simply to use <code>split</code> with only one separator <strong>by replacing in the sequence all the separators by a single one</strong> (say the first one for instance). This is the easy part; here <code>delims</code> is a variadic parameters pack holding the different delimiters:</p>
<pre><code>std::array delimiters = {delims...};

auto tokens = str
    | std::views::transform([&amp;](auto&amp;&amp; c) {
        return ((c==delims) || ...) ? delimiters[0] : c; });
}
</code></pre>
<p>Unfortunately, we can't any more build a <code>std::string_view</code> from here, so we have to deal with indexes instead and associate each character with its index in the sequence, which implies to use <code>std::views::enumerate</code> (and therefore C++23). Another issue is that <code>std::views::split</code> can't take as parameter such a couple <code>idx,character</code> as a delimiter (and there is no projection feature here), so we have to create some <code>duo</code> struct that holds this information, with an overload of <code>operator==</code> to make <code>std::views::split</code> happy</p>
<pre><code>struct duo {
    std::size_t i={};
    char c={};
    bool operator==(duo other) const { return c==other.c; }
};
</code></pre>
<p>We can then finish the definition of the <code>tokens</code> view:</p>
<pre><code>    auto tokens = str
        | std::views::transform([&amp;](auto&amp;&amp; c) {
            return ((c==delims) || ...) ? delimiters[0] : c; })
        | std::views::enumerate
        | std::views::transform([](auto t)  { return std::make_from_tuple&lt;duo&gt;(t); })
        | std::views::split (duo{0,delimiters[0]})
        | std::views::filter([](auto&amp;&amp; rng) { return !rng.empty(); })
        | std::views::transform([&amp;](auto&amp;&amp; rng) {
            return std::string_view(
                str.begin()+ rng[0].i,
                str.begin()+ rng[0].i + rng.size()
            );  })
        ;
</code></pre>
<p>We can use it as follows with 4 different delimiters for instance</p>
<pre><code>int main() {
    std::string line{&quot;\tSplit   \t\n\t   this sequence\t of  ; tokens  &quot;};
    auto tokens = tokenize_with_ranges&lt;' ','\t','\n',';'&gt;(line);
    for (const auto&amp; token : tokens) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; token &lt;&lt; '\n';
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/dPEY9eeKh" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<hr />
<p>Now, here is the <a href="https://quick-bench.com/q/V4ks07qnYOCUb7-GVqnqHP3XWkc" rel="nofollow noreferrer">benchmark part</a>, where a <code>BM_TokenizeWithRangesGeneric</code> entry for this templated <code>tokenize_with_ranges</code> has been added (with 4 delimiters in the example).</p>
<p>It is almost as fast as the <code>BM_TokenizeWithFind</code> version, and it should not depend too much on the number of delimiters in terms of performance.</p>
<p>A <code>BM_TokenizeWithRangesChunk</code> entry with <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79883058/2229039">the nice solution</a> from Jerry Coffin has also been added, with somewhat average performance (because of <code>std::views::chunk_by</code> ?)</p>
<p>The same benchmark <a href="https://quick-bench.com/q/s06ghS4QNDVTdxohXVK4fFX4an4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> without the looooong <code>regex</code> version.</p>
